{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786504421,"nonce":"YQ17GmzZ+kHKqBjwUH/h6JW0ow4=","target":"0869f213094aa9d7d10a0e7bd8f12c1c1188c01693b9fa4050a5134b9117a1fd","message":"**What is wrong.** `internal/sources/service` is 1 996 lines with no `Create`, `Update` or `Delete` of any kind. Every method on `*Service` is a read or a probe: `Get`, `OrgForSource`, `List`, `Health`, `Alerts`, `AlertGroups`, `Silences`, `Silence`, `Status`, `Rules`, `ResolveRule`, `Probe`. The entire write path for the module's own aggregate lives in `internal/sources/api`, which declares `SourceRegistry`, `ClusterRegistry`, `CredentialWriter`, `IngestTokenIssuer` and `UnitOfWork` as ports and has the composition root satisfy the first two with repositories (`internal/app/assertions.go:103-104`, wired at `internal/app/container.go:842-857`).\n\nCONTEXT.md §5 rule 3: *\"`service` imports `domain` and its own port interfaces. Concretes are injected by `internal/app/container.go`.\"* Rule 1 is *\"`api` MUST NOT import `repository`.\"* The letter of rule 1 holds — `api` imports the interface, not the package — while the intent it protects does not.\n\n**The demonstration.** `internal/sources/api/ports.go:38-45` documents the inversion itself: *\"⚠️ ARCHITECTURAL NOTE. CONTEXT.md §5.1 says `api` calls `service`, and this port is the exception that proves it: `sources/service` is deliberately read-only (there is no write path into a cluster, R3) and has no Create, Update or Delete. Rather than inventing business logic in a handler, this layer declares the narrow registry port it needs and the composition root injects the repository.\"*\n\nThe justification does not survive contact with what the handlers do. `createSource` at `internal/sources/api/sources.go:159-174` runs three writes across two modules' tables inside `rt.inTx` — seal a credential into `channel_credentials`, insert into `alert_sources`, mint a row in identity's `api_tokens` — and decides their ordering and their failure semantics. `updateSource` at `:263-284` runs a second such transaction, and inside it makes the business decision that a supplied credential rotates in place rather than being replaced (`:264-277`), reading the existing source first to find the credential id. That is multi-table orchestration with an ordering invariant, and it is the thing rule 3 exists to keep out of a handler.\n\nThe \"no write path into a cluster, R3\" argument also conflates two different things. R3 and CONTEXT.md §4's *\"`silences` | PERIPHERAL | Read-only mirror of AM silences. **No write path.**\"* are about not writing into somebody else's Alertmanager. They say nothing about oto's own `alert_sources` table, which these handlers write four ways.\n\nThe ports file already names the exit: *\"the day a `sources/service` write facade exists, it satisfies this port unchanged\"* (`ports.go:44-45`). Nothing in the current shape blocks that; the facade simply was not written.\n\n**Why it matters.** The transaction boundary is now an HTTP concern. Anything that is not an HTTP request — a job, a CLI, a future gRPC surface, a test that wants to create a source without a router — cannot create a source at all without re-deriving the ordering in `createSource` and the rotation rule in `updateSource`. That re-derivation is how the second half of this pair happened: `deleteSource` sits in the same file, skips `inTx` entirely, and commits twice (tracked separately). One handler getting the transaction right and its neighbour getting it wrong is the predictable outcome of putting the boundary where there is no single owner for it.\n\n**Where.** `internal/sources/service/` (1 996 lines, read-only), `internal/sources/api/ports.go:36-54` (`SourceRegistry` and its architectural note), `:56-68` (`ClusterRegistry`), `:70-85` (`CredentialWriter`), `:87-100` (`IngestTokenIssuer`), `:102-113` (`UnitOfWork`), `internal/sources/api/sources.go:159-174` (`createSource`'s transaction) and `:263-284` (`updateSource`'s), `internal/app/container.go:842-857` (the repositories injected as the write side), `internal/app/assertions.go:103-104`.\n\n**Scope.** medium\n\n**Done when.** `sources/service` grows the write facade `ports.go:44-45` anticipates — create, update, soft-delete and the credential/token orchestration, owning the transaction — and satisfies `SourceRegistry` unchanged, so `internal/app/container.go` swaps the injected repository for the service and the `⚠️ ARCHITECTURAL NOTE` is deleted rather than amended. No handler in `internal/sources/api` calls `rt.inTx`.","files":null}]}